The fresh positive proof does close (`#Top`, exit 0), but the real-program pinning gate has failed. The claim starts from a manually populated scope and an embedded function body; it never executes `Module(...)` from `solution.mpy` and has no auxiliary theorem connecting module loading to that scope. A material replacement of the submitted program body with `% 2.0` changes the real result at `3.5` from `0.5` to `1.5`, yet the unchanged candidate proof still returns `#Top`. This is an adequacy failure, not an unsound K rule claim.

I’ll still complete the required static inventory and independent non-vacuity test before issuing the verdict.
